          "derivation": "A Middle Iranian continuant of Av. jafra- is certainly there, but the shape depends on the dialect. Book Pahlavi has zwpl = zofr 'deep' (MacKenzie, PHD p.98: 'zofr [zwpl | M zwwpr, J zwrf, (N zarf)] deep'), with the regular Southwest-Iranian development *ǰ- > z-. The form with initial ž- and the sequence -afr, i.e. žafr, is Parthian: Manichaean Parthian 𐫋𐫜𐫡 jfr /žafr/ 'deep' (so Wiktionary s.v. ژرف, which derives NP žarf from a Middle Persian *žarf borrowed from that Parthian word, 'displacing native zofr'). Horn (Grundriss no. 685, p.152) writes the Middle Iranian ancestor as 'phlv. zufar, zafr'; Mo'in's footnote in Borhan (p.1059ff.) writes 'پهلوی zafr'. So Nourai's 'Pahlavi žafr' is a real form, but the ž- belongs to Parthian (and to New Persian), not to Book Pahlavi, where it is z-.",
